Rule O1 (doc/scope_model.mld, sections 3.1 and 7): every root keeps its origin,
the unit whose source it came from. This file tests two operations of section
7 that store roots: writing and reading a unit, and caching a lookup. A root
left unresolved in a [.odoc] file must keep its origin, so that it can be
resolved at link. A cache must not give a root of one origin the answer found
for a root of the same name with another origin.

  $ cat ext1/ext.mli
  type t = A
  $ cat ext2/ext.mli
  type t = B
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -c ext1/ext.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -c ext2/ext.mli
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/ext1 -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 ext1/ext.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/ext2 -L ext2:h/pkg/ext2 ext2/ext.cmti

How to read [odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents]. Each line
is one item of the signature. [x/0] is the item [x], with a number odoc adds
to tell apart items of the same name. [resolved(...)] is a resolved path, and
[root(pkg/x/X)] is the unit [X] of the library [x] in the package [pkg].
[unresolvedroot(X__Hid)] is a root that is not resolved yet. [hidden(...)]
marks a path to a hidden unit, and [canonical(p,resolved(q))] a path [p]
whose canonical path is [q]. [odoc_print --refs] prints each reference in
the doc comments. [r(...)] is a resolved reference, and [alias(p,q)] is the
path [q], which is an alias to [p].

O.11. Write and read a unit. [x] is a dune wrapped library built against
[ext1], with one member, [Hid]. Its alias module [X], generated by dune, has
[Hid = X__Hid], a pure alias, so [X] records no digest for [X__Hid] (rule P2,
section 3.2). [xv] is a vendored copy of [x], built from the same sources,
as when two packages each vendor one library. [z] is built against [x] and
[ext1], and has [M = X.Hid]. No unit sees both [x] and [xv] (D3, section 2).

  $ cat x/x.ml-gen
  (** @canonical X.Hid *)
  module Hid = X__Hid
  $ cat xv/x.ml-gen
  (** @canonical X.Hid *)
  module Hid = X__Hid
  $ cat x/hid.mli
  module E = Ext
  $ cat xv/hid.mli
  module E = Ext
  $ cat z/z.mli
  module M = X.Hid
  $ for l in x xv; do
  >   ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -w -49 -I $l -I ext1 -o $l/x -c -impl $l/x.ml-gen
  >   ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I $l -I ext1 -open X -o $l/x__Hid -c -intf $l/hid.mli
  > done
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I z -I x -I ext1 -c z/z.mli

The alias module depends on no other unit, so a driver may compile it with
odoc before its members. Then the target of [Hid] is not compiled yet, and
[Hid] should be left for link (section 7):

  $ for l in x xv; do
  >   odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/$l -L $l:h/pkg/$l -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 $l/x.cmt
  >   odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/$l -L $l:h/pkg/$l -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 $l/x__Hid.cmti
  > done
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/z -L z:h/pkg/z -L x:h/pkg/x -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 z/z.cmti
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/x/x.odoc
  module Hid/0 = unresolvedroot(X__Hid)

Both [x] and [xv] are given at link. The root [X__Hid] was read back from
[X]'s file, and its origin is [X], which was compiled with [x] only. So it
should mean [x]'s [X__Hid], with no warning:

  $ link() { odoc link -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 -L ext2:h/pkg/ext2 -L x:h/pkg/x -L xv:h/pkg/xv -L z:h/pkg/z -L a:h/pkg/a -L b:h/pkg/b -L w:h/pkg/w "$@"; }
  $ link h/pkg/x/x.odoc
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/x/x.odocl
  module Hid/0 = resolved(hidden(root(pkg/x/X__Hid))) 

[z]'s link reads [X]'s [.odoc] file, not the linked one, to resolve
[X.Hid]. The root [X__Hid] in it still has the origin [X], so [M] should be
[x]'s [X__Hid]. Its canonical tag [X.Hid] is a reference (S1), and both [x]
and [xv] have an [X] in Λl, so the tag is ambiguous: odoc should warn, and
leave it unresolved (S2). An author would write [/x/X.Hid]; dune does not
write tags with a library yet.

A resolved tag, to either library's [X.Hid], would be wrong.

  $ link h/pkg/z/z.odoc
  File "X":
  Ambiguous lookup. Possible units: pkg/x/X, pkg/xv/X
  $ odoc_print --short --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/z/z.odocl
  module M/0 =
    resolved(canonical(root(pkg/x/X).Hid,resolved(root(pkg/xv/X).Hid)))

O.10. A cache keyed by origin. [a] is built against [ext1] and [b] against
[ext2]. Each has the pure alias [E = Ext], so the root text [Ext] is the same
in both, with different origins. [w] is built against nothing, and its doc
comment has the references [{!A.E.t}] and [{!B.E.t}]. These are forward
references, resolved among the libraries given at link (rule R3, section
4.2). Each library's [Λc] holds one [Ext], so D3 holds.

  $ cat a/a.mli
  module E = Ext
  $ cat b/b.mli
  module E = Ext
  $ cat w/w.mli
  (** See {!A.E.t} and {!B.E.t}. *)
  type t
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I a -I ext1 -c a/a.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I b -I ext2 -c b/b.mli
  $ ocamlc -bin-annot -no-alias-deps -I w -c w/w.mli
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/a -L a:h/pkg/a -L ext1:h/pkg/ext1 a/a.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/b -L b:h/pkg/b -L ext2:h/pkg/ext2 b/b.cmti
  $ odoc compile --output-dir h --parent-id pkg/w -L w:h/pkg/w w/w.cmti

Projection meets the root [Ext] twice while [w] is linked, in one run of
odoc. Each root follows section 3 with its own origin, so [A.E.t] should be
[ext1]'s [Ext.t], and [B.E.t] should be [ext2]'s, with no warning:

  $ link h/pkg/w/w.odoc
  $ odoc_print --refs --long-paths --show-root-parents h/pkg/w/w.odocl
  r(alias(root(pkg/ext1/Ext),root(pkg/a/A).E).t)
  r(alias(root(pkg/ext2/Ext),root(pkg/b/B).E).t)
